the predator gets a makeover and gets  outfoxed by a dog prey is more than just  predator 5. here's how the prequel kept  the franchise fresh  in predator 2 the predatorship features  artifacts and weapons spanning many  times in cultures as a result each new  predator film can really pit the  creature against any group of humans  enter prey which follows comanche  warrior naru as she battles european  soldiers and the predator in north  america in 1719 in prey our beloved  predator is traveling light on a  vacation to earth while the humanoid  alien is shown using its invisibility  cloak mode on multiple occasions the  creature doesn't rely on any of its  other gadgetry as much as it has in  previous films that isn't to say that  all of the high-tech alien weapons are  absent merely that this heiress predator  prefers to stab and impale its prey  rather than blast them to smithereens it  fought and killed a bear with its hands  since the film does take place 300 years  ago it's possible that this version of  the predator is not quite fully evolved  in the weapons department while the  alien hunter is certainly far more  advanced than humanity in 1719 it  doesn't appear to have all the tricks  and upgrades of alien vs predator more  importantly this makes it a much fairer  contest between the humans and the alien  creature since it isn't drawing upon an  endless arsenal of options however it  would be interesting to witness how the  creature fares without the use of its  cloak technology at all since that still  allows it to sneak up on humans animals  at least can sniff the creature out even  if they can't see the predator  the success of john mctiernan's predator  film can be attributed to its survival  horror elements dutch and his team are  out in a jungle surrounded by trees  constricting the space puts them at the  mercy of the alien whose use of  camouflage and the environment makes  members of the group sitting ducks  similarly other predator movies  especially the good ones that try to  honor the original rely on this key  element as the alien has a place to hide  sneak and strike from to borrow a terry  silver quote from the karate kid part 3  a man can't see he can't fight  prey features some similar situations to  the first predator where there are  moments of confinement but it also poses  a new question in asking how good the  predator is in open spaces the great  plains boast vast amounts of open land  with only grass in the distance in  theory this would be the best place to  lure the alien creature to since it  removes the high ground aspect and lets  any sudden movement be more identifiable  however prey confirms that the creature  is as lethal in this setting as in  others  one especially memorable scene finds the  beast chasing naru and a fellow comanche  warrior across an open field a set piece  reminiscent of the velociraptors  stalking the hunters through the long  grass in the lost world jurassic park  the predators hunt other life forms for  both sport and pride claiming  ritualistic trophies from their  victories they want to prove their  superiority quite often in the most  brutal way imaginable while maintaining  a code of honor for their prey this code  is questionable though since it isn't  even a fair fight if the opponent fails  to have intergalactic weapons in their  possession so it goes without saying  that their moral compass is a bit skewed  in other predator films the audience  witnesses the alien hunter go after  humans as their main target identifying  them as worthy adversaries ironically  humans aren't even considered apex  predators in our own world since we  don't eat everything we destroy but in  prey the predator goes after several  creatures not just humans it faces off  in two big contests against a wolf and a  bear the wolf shows its nose is key in  this encounter however it proves to be  no match for the predator's sharp blades  on the other hand the bear strikes some  real blows to the creature if the  predator's cloaking ability had been  removed there's a good chance it might  have struggled to deal with the bear's  sheer power  next to its age-old nemesis the  xenomorph the predator's design is one  of cinema's most distinctive and iconic  creatures from its trademark dreadlocks  to its seriously gruesome face the  universe traversing hunter would be  pretty easy to pick out in a lineup  however in prey the predator looks a  little different  showcasing a leaner gladiator aesthetic  while it's still easily identifiable it  did receive a makeover with a few tweaks  to its physical appearance that fans  noticed immediately dan trachtenberg  explained why he felt it was necessary  to change the creature's appearance for  the film telling time out i wanted to  make sure the head was more proportional  to the body this predator is much  slimmer and less armored than it's ever  been it's more creature it's still  hulking and ferocious at san diego  comic-con trachtenberg also revealed  that he and his team included goop  around the predator's face to make it  more disturbing to look at  in predator 2 the creature navigates a  concrete jungle with relative ease and  fluidity despite the unique surroundings  of los angeles it understands what it  needs to do to hunt and survive in this  setting the reason for this is likely  due to the events of prey which serve as  an important lesson for all the other  predators of what not to do when on  earth in prey the predator falls into a  quicksand style mud trap which  ultimately plays a major part in its  downfall like anyone else in such a  pickle it struggles to get out of this  situation especially as it had been  injured beforehand which made its  predicament even worse but it does lead  one to wonder if perhaps quicksand  didn't exist on any of the other planets  the predator had visited before it's  also possible that this predator is  still young and inexperienced in its  hunts maybe earth is its first rite of  passage and it's trying to prove itself  quickly and recklessly that would also  explain why the creature fights the bear  in the river when the water isn't  conducive to its cloak mode's  effectiveness and helps to reveal its  presence  throughout all the predator films the  alien has taken on many formidable foes  who have proven to be worthy adversaries  and even victors from the terrifying  xenomorph to walking muscle mountain  dutch shaffer played by arnold  schwarzenegger the alien hunter has  faced off against the best of the best  across the planet in prey the predator  gets too cocky for its own good failing  to properly determine all of its threats  not only does the predator dismiss  naaru's abilities but it also overlooks  her dog while the predator manages to  put down a wolf a bear and countless  other human beings throughout the movie  it eventually falls to the outstanding  teamwork of naaru and her dog one could  even argue that it's the dog that makes  the difference here as naru is left  exposed in the final fight on two  occasions after previously saving the  comanche warrior from the predator the  four-legged mutt appears at the end of  the film with an axe when naru needs it  most in many ways naru's faithful friend  is the true hero of this story  remaining quiet is often the key to  successfully capturing prey while  hunting since it helps to maintain the  element of surprise allowing the hunter  to strike hard and fast in the previous  predator movies silence hasn't always  been possible while everyone tries to  keep their lip sealed and their mobile  phones on silent when in danger groups  of people tend to talk or move around  even when their imminent threat of total  annihilation looms near there's  something else out there  on the other hand prey is a quiet film  besides the occasional bird chirp or  rustle of the leaves there's an intense  stillness and quiet to the great plains  in many ways this setting is ideal for  the predator as the silence ensures the  creature is able to hear the footsteps  of its prey from a mile away however  it's equally effective for the intended  victim too since any sudden movement  will alert them of a problem on the  horizon as an example naaru manages to  find and attack the unaware predator  simply because she's quieter than the  alien talk about using the hunter's  tactics against them  there's an argument to be made that the  predator franchise falls firmly under  the umbrella of horror after all the  whole concept of an almighty alien  hunter chasing after humans is  frightening plus the creature's unmasked  face is pure nightmare fuel plus the  franchise's previous films are  jam-packed with jump scares with victims  never knowing what's lurking around  every corner in prey some of those scary  elements remain but there's something  different about this version this time  the predator seems far more savage than  before this alien creature uses its raw  power and lightning speed to wicked  effect it moves with a resolute purpose  and uncanny brutality that has been  lacking in the franchise's recent  toutings while brutality is a key  element of the slasher genre the  predator isn't coming at people through  their nightmares or hiding behind doors  and walls it doesn't hide in the shadows  like a boogeyman instead it charges into  battle like a warrior that isn't afraid  of anything which is somehow more  horrifying  it's a foregone conclusion that the  better a warrior's class of weapons the  better chance they have of success in  the previous predator films the hunter's  shoulder cannons have made all the  difference in battle in prey the  creature prefers to use hand-to-hand  combat to dispose of its opponents but  it isn't averse to pulling out the big  guns when absolutely necessary but that  doesn't mean their superior weapons win  out in the end while literally stuck in  sinking mud the predator points its  weapon at naaru and fires however it  fails to realize that it isn't wearing  its helmet which features incredibly  accurate targeting capabilities it also  doesn't notice that naaru has carefully  set up the helmet to lock the predator  in its crosshairs as a result the  creature ends up on the receiving end of  its own attack again it proves that  technology can be useful in a fight but  a strategist will always trump an  aggressive brute in the end  consistent across the predator franchise  is that the extraterrestrial beings  believe in a warrior's code they don't  hunt for a tasty snack or out of  self-defense instead they take on  opponents they believe will provide a  tough daunting challenge or who would  make excellent sparring partners like in  the yakuza sword fight in predators for  these alien hunters it's about testing  their superior skills and advanced  smarts as they attempt to prove they are  worthy enough to be a part of their clan  throughout the predator films this code  seems to remain intact however prey  explores what happens if the alien isn't  actually all that good at identifying  threats or challengers for whatever  reason  this is as far as you go  undoubtedly the predator sees the  comanche warriors as worthy enough to  challenge and hunt it stalks and battles  them coming out on top on several  occasions however the creature fails to  see naaru as a threat from the get-go  she demonstrates a warrior's spirit and  natural fighting ability even if some of  her own people doubt her skills however  the predator basically scoffs at the  thought of taking her on but it's  naaru's resilience and ingenuity that  bring down the alien hunter in combat  imagine how different prey would have  been had the predator never  underestimated naru in the first place  [Music]
 

